course: books
lesson: 36
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter III, part 4: The Wild Wood (the door-scraper, the door-mat, and a green door)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the lumpy little valley the Mole trips and falls with a squeal — he's cut his shin. The Rat ties it up with his handkerchief and looks closely: a very clean cut, not from a branch — from the sharp edge of something metal. «Funny!» Then he starts digging in the snow like mad.
explain: «Hooray!» He's found a door-scraper, and dances a jig round it. The Mole, hurt and grumpy, can't see why: someone careless has left a door-scraper lying about in the wood, that's all! The Rat digs on and finds a shabby door-mat. «Can we EAT a doormat?» snaps the Mole. «Door-mats know their place.»
explain: The Rat loses his temper: stop talking and dig, especially on the sides of the mounds — it's their last chance of a warm dry bed! The Mole digs, mostly to humour his friend, who he thinks is going a bit mad.
explain: The Rat's stick hits something hollow. They dig — and there, in the side of a snow-bank, is a solid little dark-green door with an iron bell-pull and a brass plate: MR. BADGER.
explain: The Mole falls over backwards in delight and praises the Rat's clever step-by-step thinking at great length. «But as you haven't» got my brains, snaps the Rat, «get up and ring that bell!» The Mole swings on the bell-pull, the Rat hammers with his stick, and far away a deep bell rings.
fact: The Mole tripped and cried «O my leg! ... O my poor shin!»; the Rat tied it up with his handkerchief.
fact: The Rat: «It's a very clean cut ... That was never done by a branch or a stump. Looks as if it was made by a sharp edge of something in metal. Funny!»
fact: «"Well, never mind what done it," said the Mole, forgetting his grammar in his pain.» — correct English would be «what did it».
fact: The Rat cried «Hooray!» and then «Hooray-oo-ray-oo-ray-oo-ray!» and «fell to executing a feeble jig in the snow» (a jig = a lively hopping dance).
fact: The Mole: «A door-scraper! Well, what of it? Why dance jigs around a door-scraper?» — a door-scraper is a metal bar by a front door for scraping mud off your boots.
fact: The Mole thought it simply meant «some VERY careless and forgetful person has left his door-scraper lying about in the middle of the Wild Wood» and said he'd complain «to—to somebody or other».
fact: «O, dear! O, dear!» cried the Rat, «in despair at his obtuseness» (obtuseness = being slow to understand).
fact: When the door-mat appeared — «There, what did I tell you?» — the Mole answered «Absolutely nothing whatever ... with perfect truthfulness»: the Rat really hadn't told him anything.
fact: The Mole: «Can we EAT a doormat? or sleep under a door-mat? Or sit on a door-mat and sledge home over the snow on it, you exasperating rodent?» and «Door-mats know their place.» (exasperating = very annoying).
fact: The Rat had asked: «Do—you—mean—to—say ... that this door-mat doesn't TELL you anything?» Then, «really angry»: «Now look here, you—you thick-headed beast ... this must stop. Not another word, but scrape—scrape and scratch and dig and hunt round, especially on the sides of the hummocks, if you want to sleep dry and warm to-night, for it's our last chance!»
fact: The Mole dug «more to oblige the Rat than for any other reason, for his opinion was that his friend was getting light-headed» (light-headed = a bit dizzy or mad).
fact: «the point of the Rat's cudgel struck something that sounded hollow»; then they found «a solid-looking little door, painted a dark green. An iron bell-pull hung by the side, and below it, on a small brass plate ... MR. BADGER.»
fact: The Mole: «You argued it out, step by step, in that wise head of yours, from the very moment that I fell and cut my shin, and you looked at the cut, and at once your majestic mind said to itself, "Door-scraper!"» and «If I only had your head, Ratty——». The Rat's chain: a clean metal cut → a door-scraper → a door-mat → a door.
fact: The Rat: «But as you haven't, I suppose you're going to sit on the snow all night and talk? Get up at once and hang on to that bell-pull». The Mole swung on it «both feet well off the ground», and «from quite a long way off they could faintly hear a deep-toned bell respond».
fact: Old words: «pettishly» = sulkily; «folly» = silliness; «hitherto incredulous» = until now not believing; «penitence» = being sorry; «appreciated» = valued; «domestic litter» = household rubbish.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «big brain» = clever; «galaxy brain» = so clever it's silly (said as a joke); «eejit» (Ireland) = idiot; «gutted» (UK) = very disappointed; «sorted» (UK) = fixed, done; «tidy» (Wales) = good, great.
exercise: What happens to the Mole in the dell? | He trips and cuts his shin | He falls into a pond | He finds a door | He sees a weasel
exercise: What does the Rat tie the cut up with? | His handkerchief | A strip torn from his coat | A wad of dry grass | A bandage from his pocket
exercise: What does the Rat say about the cut? | Very clean, made by something metal | Very messy, from a branch | Nothing | A weasel bit him
exercise: «never mind what done it». What is wrong with the Mole's words? | It should be «what did it» | He should say «who», not «what», as an animal did it | Nothing is wrong | Too long
exercise: What does the Rat find first? | A door-scraper | A door-mat | A green door | A bell-pull
exercise: What does the Rat do when he finds it? | A feeble jig in the snow | Sits down and cries for joy | Shouts for Badger | Throws it away
exercise: What is a door-scraper for? | Scraping mud off your boots | Scraping snow off a door | Opening doors | Cleaning windows
exercise: What does the Mole think the door-scraper means? | Someone careless left it lying about | That someone's house is near | Nothing | It's Toad's
exercise: Who does the Mole say he'll complain to? | «somebody or other» | Badger | The police in Town, about careless people | Toad
exercise: What does the Rat find second? | A shabby door-mat | A small brass plate with a name engraved on it in capitals | A small dark cave mouth | An old tin lantern
exercise: «There, what did I tell you?» — what does the Mole answer? | «Absolutely nothing whatever» | «You were right all along, Ratty» | «A door-mat» | «Badger»
exercise: Which is NOT one of the Mole's grumpy questions about the door-mat? | «Can we RIDE a door-mat?» | «Can we EAT a doormat?» | «or sleep under a door-mat?» | «Or sit on a door-mat and sledge home?»
exercise: «Door-mats know their place.» What does the Mole mean? | Door-mats don't «tell» anyone anything | Door-mats belong in Toad Hall | Mats can talk | Mats are rude
exercise: What does the Rat call the Mole when he gets really angry? | «you thick-headed beast» | «you silly ass» | «my young friend» | «old fellow»
exercise: Where does the Rat say to dig? | On the sides of the hummocks | Under the beech tree where the Mole hid | In the middle of the dell | Anywhere
exercise: Why does the Mole dig? | To please the Rat | He understands | He's cold | He's hungry
exercise: What does the Mole think about the Rat now? | He's getting light-headed | He's a genius who knows exactly what he's doing | He's tired | He's lost
exercise: What does the Rat's cudgel strike? | Something hollow | A big round rock hidden under the snow | A thick tree root | A sheet of thick ice
exercise: What colour is the door? | Dark green | Canary-yellow with red trim, like Toad's caravan | Bright blue | Muddy brown
exercise: What hangs by the side of the door? | An iron bell-pull | A lantern | A knocker | A sign saying «Private»
exercise: What is written on the brass plate? | MR. BADGER | TOAD HALL | PRIVATE | MR. RAT
exercise: What does the Mole do when he sees the door? | Falls backwards in delight | Runs away into the wood in fright, thinking it's a trap set by the weasels | Knocks | Cries
exercise: How does the Mole ring the bell? | He swings on the bell-pull | He presses a little brass button beside the door again and again | He shouts | He throws snow
exercise: «hitherto incredulous Mole». What does «incredulous» mean? | Not believing | Very believing | Feeling very tired | Shivering with cold
test: Ella, absolutely gutted (very disappointed): «the Mole worked out the door, the Rat just tagged along.» Put it right. | The Rat worked it out; the Mole thought he'd gone mad | Right: the Mole saw the door-scraper and knew at once it meant Badger's house was right there | They both guessed | Badger did
test: Why does the Rat's jig puzzle the Mole? | He doesn't see what the door-scraper means | He's never seen anyone dance in his whole life | He hates dancing | It's too fast
test: The Rat asks «There, what did I tell you?» and the Mole says «Absolutely nothing whatever». Why is the Mole right, in a way? | The Rat hadn't explained his idea at all | The Rat had told him everything clearly, step by step | The Mole wasn't listening | The Rat lost his voice
test: «Can we EAT a doormat? ... you exasperating rodent?» What is the Mole feeling? | Hurt, tired and fed up | Happy and grateful | Hungry for doormats | Sleepy
test: Rory, tidy (great) idea: «the Rat should just have explained it to the Mole». Would that have helped? | Yes — the Mole only grumbled because he didn't see the plan | No: the Mole could never understand anything, however carefully it was explained | No: it was a secret | It doesn't matter
test: How does the Rat's thinking go? | Metal cut → scraper → mat → door | Snow means winter, and in winter Badger is always asleep in bed, so they must wake him | Faces mean weasels | A hunch with no clues
test: The Mole praises the Rat: «You ought to go where you'll be properly appreciated». How does the Rat answer? | He tells him to stop talking and ring the bell | He thanks him warmly, and they shake paws and talk about it for a while in the snow | «Yes, to Town» | Nothing
test: What would have happened if the Mole hadn't cut his shin? | They might never have found the door | They would have walked home easily along the river, following their own footprints | Badger would find them | Nothing
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat said Badger «hates Society». Why might ringing his bell at night be a risk? | Badger might be cross at visitors | Badger loves late visitors | Badger is out | The bell is broken
test: Looking back: in chapter III part 1, how many times had the Rat ever called at Badger's home? | Never | Every week | Just once | Twice, in spring
test: Looking back: in chapter II the book said the Mole «had a candid nature». How does he show it here? | He says honestly what he thinks of the mat | He tells the Rat a clever lie so as not to hurt his feelings about the door-mat | He hides it | He's silent
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Mole grabbed the oars thinking «he could do it every bit as well». What does he think of the Rat's brains now? | «If I only had your head, Ratty——» | That he himself is far cleverer than the Rat and found the door first | That the Rat is silly | Toad's cleverer
test: Looking back: who has saved the Mole twice now? | The Rat | The Otter, from the river, and then Badger, from the Wild Wood | Toad, both times | Nobody at all
test: Looking back: chapter III began with the Mole wanting to meet Badger. Where has it brought him? | To Badger's front door | To Toad Hall, where Toad is polishing his new motor-car | Back home | The Wide World
test: Looking back: in chapter II the Rat said of Toad «Perhaps he's not very clever». Who is shown to be clever here? | The Rat | Mr. Toad | The Mole | The horse
test: ★★★ The Mole retells the Rat's thinking step by step. According to him, which word did the Rat's «majestic mind» say to itself first? | «Door-scraper!» | «Door-mat!» | «Badger lives somewhere near here, so let's look!» | «Snow!»
test: ★★★ Alice (lessons 1–24) also met puzzles: the Caterpillar's «Who are YOU?», the Hatter's riddle with no answer. How is the Rat's puzzle different? | It has a real answer, found from clues | It has no answer at all, like the raven and the writing-desk | It's a pun | It's a song
test: ★★★ «door-scraper», «door-mat», «door». Which is the odd one out, and why? | The door: it was found by reasoning from the other two | The door-mat: it is the only one of the three that is made of metal | The scraper | None
exam: Identify the inference drawn by the Rat from the nature of the wound sustained by the Mole. | That it was made by a sharp edge of metal | That it had been inflicted by a weasel or stoat lying in wait beneath the snow | Nothing | That it was ice
exam: Ascertain the interpretation placed by the Mole upon the discovery of the door-scraper. | That a careless person had left it lying about | That a dwelling lay close at hand | That it belonged to Toad | That the snow had ceased
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the inscription upon the brass plate. | MR. BADGER, in square capital letters | PRIVATE, NO LANDING ALLOWED | TOAD HALL | No inscription whatsoever was present
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the conclusion of the third chapter? | The Mole swung on the bell-pull, and a deep bell was heard | Badger opened the door at once and welcomed them warmly into his kitchen | They went home | The Rat rang alone
